  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Treasury obligated $6B in FY2026 to date. The Department of the Treasury issued 7,653 awards with an average award size of $778K. Obligations are down 30.4% year over year. Yearly obligations show a decline from prior fiscal peaks, with FY21 at $9.6B, FY23 at $10.3B, and FY25 at $8.6B before the FY26 total of $6B. The Department of the Treasury concentrated FY2026 to date spending in a small set of NAICS categories and vendors: Nonferrous Metal (except Aluminum) Smelting and Refining received $1.4B and Other Computer Related Services received $1B, while STONEX FINANCIAL LTD received $1.2B and ASAHI REFINING USA INC received $574M. This mix reflects large single-category commitments in metals and a sizable information technology and services footprint, alongside a vendor list that includes both commodity processors and federal IT services providers. The totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What did Treasury obligate in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of the Treasury obligated $6B in FY2026 to date. The Department of the Treasury made 7,653 awards with an average award size of $778K. Obligations are down 30.4% year over year. The Department of the Treasury's FY26 total is lower than FY25's $8.6B and well below FY23's $10.3B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries received the most money?",
        "body": "Nonferrous Metal (except Aluminum) Smelting and Refining is the largest NAICS category at $1.4B, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $1B and Computer Systems Design Services at $655M. STONEX FINANCIAL LTD is the largest vendor at $1.2B, followed by ASAHI REFINING USA INC at $574M and ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $267M. The Department of the Treasury's top categories include both metals processing and IT services, and the top vendors include financial processors, refiners, and federal IT contractors."
      }
    ]
